00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.283
But if we focus in
on one aspect of what

00:02:43.283 --> 00:02:45.450
we're going to talk about
today, which is rendering.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.810
Rendering is, for those of you
who are not familiar with it,

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:51.630
it's the process of taking--

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.650
how do you define
rendering in one sentence?

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:58.950
The process of taking a 3D set,
an environment and characters

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.770
and assets and
textures and generating

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:05.110
an image, a still, 2D, two
dimensional image out of it.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.770
And remember that films are
made up of a bunch of frames

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:11.910
and they play back at
24 frames per second.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:15.780
And each frame might take
minutes or hours or even

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.530
days to render.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.970
There's one example, some
frames from Pixar's "Inside Out"

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.620
took up to 33 hours to render.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.245
A single frame.

00:03:23.245 --> 00:03:24.578
YANNICK GUILLERM: That's insane.

00:03:24.578 --> 00:03:26.480
ADRIAN GRAHAM: And that's
just one iteration.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.685
Artists tend to
iterate over and over.

00:03:28.685 --> 00:03:30.060
So they have all
these strategies

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.493
for sort of faking it and
making it seem like a lot--

00:03:33.493 --> 00:03:35.910
like they'll do low res or
they'll do low quality and then

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.220
they'll just only crank
up the quality of the end,

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.970
but that's a 33-hour render.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:41.970
But then when you think
about the total number

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.490
of compute hours required
to generate everything

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:50.320
in a whole film, you know,
172,000 frames is many,

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.300
many core hours.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.250
Over 5 million core hours.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.650
And again, that's only
if you render it once.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.570
Typically artists will
iterate over and over

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.440
until they get something that--

00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:59.470
YANNICK GUILLERM:
Until they like it.

00:03:59.470 --> 00:03:59.970
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Right, yeah.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:01.860
I mean, Pixar is pretty
crazy because they

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:03.780
render everything in frame.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.343
That means that every shot is--

00:04:06.343 --> 00:04:08.260
there's a smaller amount
of things being done,

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.650
but other film like
"Avengers," they'll

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.000
render individual elements.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.557
Like the character will
be rendered separately

00:04:14.557 --> 00:04:15.390
from the background.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:18.953
So those are separate renders
and separate processes.

00:04:18.953 --> 00:04:20.370
So how do you
handle all that data

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:24.720
and how do you farm it
out to your render farm?

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.660
We partnered with Sony
Picture Image Works

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.930
to open source their
cue management system.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:31.920
Cue management
system is essentially

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.030
a job manager and a scheduler
that can push out data and keep

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.990
track of resources and feed
jobs to individual resources

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:42.480
on a very strict
dependency type scale

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.090
so that you've got jobs that
are dependent on other jobs that

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.280
need to finish before this job
starts, et cetera, et cetera.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.370
That's what a cue
manager's really good at.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:54.705
So Sony Image Works developed
this over the last 15 years,

00:04:54.705 --> 00:04:56.580
and they partnered with
us to open source it.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.110
So we've stripped
out all of their--

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.320
worked with them to strip
out their proprietary

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.030
or other external dependencies
and it's available for free

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:07.225
on GitHub.

00:05:07.225 --> 00:05:08.600
And there's
documentation online.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.000
Now the reason I'm
mentioning this is

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.047
