So--

00:05:47.294 --> 00:05:50.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.240
When we talk about legacy
storage architecture,

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.502
I want to look at what's
hitting the shared file

00:05:55.502 --> 00:05:56.960
server, what you
see in the middle,

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:58.160
in this type of workload.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.990
And so there's
artist workstations--

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:01.580
and we're not talking
about anything on the cloud

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:01.940
right now.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:03.357
We're just talking
about everybody

00:06:03.357 --> 00:06:06.165
with a really expensive
workstation under their desk.

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:08.540
And they're loading files and
they're reading and writing

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:10.880
assets and they're talking
to their asset management

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:12.440
database, and they're
deploying jobs

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:14.510
to things like the
render workers.

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:16.730
And render farms can
typically, depending

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.700
on the size of the company--

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:20.660
I think WETA, which is
in New Zealand, which

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.768
does the Hobbit films and all
the Lord of the Rings films--

00:06:24.768 --> 00:06:27.060
they have one of the largest
render farms in the world.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.650
I think it's something in
the order of 120,000 cores.

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:33.590
So obviously each
machine, each blade

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.745
is made up of a
few of those cores.

00:06:35.745 --> 00:06:37.370
There might be eight
cores or 16 cores,

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:39.120
but you've got thousands
of render workers

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.180
all hitting storage
at the same time.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:43.700
And in render
workloads, typically,

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:45.350
at the start of
the process they'll

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:48.020
read a whole bunch
of data into memory

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:50.492
and then they'll sit there
and compute in memory.

00:06:50.492 --> 00:06:51.950
And then when
they're finished they

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:53.720
write a finished
rendered frame which

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:55.170
gets written back to the disk.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:56.390
Now that's just for rendered.

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:58.880
If you think about things
like liquid simulations

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.830
or fluid dynamic
simulations, those

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:02.120
are generating cache data.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.590
And that's every frame,
they're generating

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.710
possibly gigabytes
or even terabytes

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:10.580
of data, which is not unique
across different workloads

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:12.980
because if you think about
scientific visualization

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:15.320
and oil and gas
exploration, they also

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:19.340
generate massive amounts
of simulation cache data

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.890
that could in turn be read by
the people on the workstations

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:23.772
or by the render farm.

00:07:23.772 --> 00:07:25.730
There's a lot of transfer
going back and forth.

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:28.640
In the olden days,
back when I had hair,

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:30.290
we had multiple
facilities that needed

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:33.920
to synchronize between
on prem locations,

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:39.410
possibly around the US or
possibly even across to the UK

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:41.588
or to Asia.

00:07:41.588 --> 00:07:43.130
So there's a lot of
transfer and sync

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:45.090
data coming in and out there.

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:48.320
And finally, if you've got
lots of assets and lots

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:50.090
of production data
to manage, you're

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:52.250
going to need some sort
of management database.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.800
And that may not be reading
and writing massive amounts,

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.070
but it's many hundreds
or thousands of QPS,

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:01.490
queries per second, that
is hitting the storage

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:04.670
to do things like file
comparisons and asking

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:08.165
has this file changed since
the last time it was read,

00:08:08.165 --> 00:08:10.790
and maybe there needs to be some
sort of front-end cache there.

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:14.660
So to summarize, you need
scalability and durability.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:17.030
Also, don't forget you
need to provide things

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:18.687
like snapshots so that--

00:08:18.687 --> 00:08:19.770
and I've done this before.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:22.430
I accidentally deleted a
file on a Linux system.

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.650
There's no trash folder
on a Linux workstation.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:27.260
So how do you get
that file back?

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:29.570
You can go into the snapshot
folder and pull it back.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:31.790
I've done that countless times.

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:34.280
But then you also need
to think about archive.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:35.990
What's happening at
the end of a film

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:40.100
is they'll sort of
clean all of the assets

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:42.799
up and make sure to get rid of
any data that they don't need,

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:43.610
and then they'll archive it.

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:45.568
And they might need to
restore it at some point

00:08:45.568 --> 00:08:47.300
if they've got a
sequel of a film,

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.520
that they'll need to pull
an existing asset down.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.160
So that needs to be highly
available and easy to find

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.510
and searchable.

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:57.140
Disaster recovery, as well.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:02.045
And a lot of customers are
concerned about regionalization

00:09:02.045 --> 00:09:02.920
of disaster recovery.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:04.628
They want it somewhere
completely outside

00:09:04.628 --> 00:09:05.710
of the existing region.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:08.300
We'll talk about that in a bit.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.050
And snapshotting, we
talked about earlier.

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:12.640
But there's also sync
mechanisms as well.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:14.620
And this just sort of
gets a little deeper

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:18.520
into the notion of
multiple facilities

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:21.130
that need access to
the same data that

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:24.620
aren't too far apart for both
sides to read the same storage.

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:26.530
So you can have multiple
storage appliances

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:27.700
all synchronizing together.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:31.600
That's an important
feature as well.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.600
Migrating data from on prem
supports more hybrid workflows.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.557
So if you've got on
prem infrastructure

00:09:38.557 --> 00:09:41.140
and you're still using it, and
you want to burst to the cloud,

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:42.970
for example, you need
a way to get the data

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:46.287
from on prem to the cloud
efficiently and selectively

00:09:46.287 --> 00:09:48.370
so that you could then
render frames, for example,

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.140
on the cloud.

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:51.310
And then, of course, cost.

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:53.310
How much do you want to
spend and how performant

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:55.828
do you need it to be and
what kind of data egress

00:09:55.828 --> 00:09:56.620
are you looking at?

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:59.140
Egress, of course, the data you
need to take out of the cloud.

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:01.670
In this case, we're not going to
take any data out of the cloud,

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:02.980
but if you need to take
the rendered frames

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:04.840
and write them back
to your on prem,

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:07.330
you need to consider what
mechanism is doing that.

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:08.797
So enter Elastifile.

00:10:08.797 --> 00:10:09.880
YANNICK GUILLERM: Awesome.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:11.245
Thank you.

00:10:11.245 --> 00:10:11.870
Thanks, Adrian.

