00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:54.790
in Santa Clara.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:57.290
The HQ headquarter
is in Tel Aviv.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:59.290
So that's where
all the R and D is,

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:01.030
and we have a presence
in Europe as well,

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:03.400
in London and Amsterdam.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.860
We're really a team
of storage experts.

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:09.550
We have deep expertise into
distributed system, optimizing

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:11.920
applications, and
IOPS and throughput

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:13.470
and all the good thing.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:16.600
And we've been at Google, a
partner for quite a bit now,

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:17.920
and it's been a blast.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:21.400
It's really been
very good for us.

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:25.933
Elastifile in one sentence.

00:11:25.933 --> 00:11:27.100
It's pretty easy at the end.

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:29.900
Elastifile is a scale-out
NAS for the cloud.

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:33.503
So it's a scale-out
NAS for Google.

00:11:33.503 --> 00:11:35.170
Not all of you but
some of you, I guess,

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:38.240
use [INAUDIBLE] or NetApp
or [INAUDIBLE] on prem.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:40.697
So it's what that
is, but in Google.

00:11:40.697 --> 00:11:42.280
So it's going to
give you file access,

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:43.870
NFS file access for
your application

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:45.400
to run in the cloud.

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:48.160
So the cool thing with that is
that you can migrate and move

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:52.210
your traditional legacy
applications to the cloud

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:55.315
today, and when you do
rendering, when you do flight

00:11:55.315 --> 00:11:56.890
simulation, when you do even--

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:59.860
we did some tests
with Adobe Premiere,

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:02.450
all those applications talk
in a NFS [INAUDIBLE] system.

00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:07.513
None of them talk S3 or
the other object protocols.

00:12:07.513 --> 00:12:09.430
So you don't have to
refactor your application

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:10.555
to bring them to the cloud.

00:12:10.555 --> 00:12:12.220
It's there, it
scales, and you'll

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:14.880
see that it's supported in
pretty much every region where

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:17.380
GCP is.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:18.640
So it's a software only.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:20.350
So the cool thing
with that is that you

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:24.160
can install it in all the
regions where GCP is available.

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:26.320
And we take advantage
of all the new features

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.450
that those guys are
going to come up with.

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:30.640
New disks, new versions
of the instances

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:32.440
with better CPU and all that.

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:34.210
We don't depend on
machines that you have

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:35.510
to install in data centers.

00:12:35.510 --> 00:12:38.030
It's all using GCP resources.

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:40.342
So right out the door,
all the instances

00:12:40.342 --> 00:12:42.050
that those guys are
going to come up with

00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:43.880
are going to be
usable on our site.

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:48.490
It's Posix NFS so Google,
NFS protocol that you know.

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:49.460
No app factoring.

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:53.260
So you bring your app into GCP,
you launch an instance, a Linux

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:57.160
machine, and you install
a blender, a rendering

00:12:57.160 --> 00:12:57.760
application.

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:00.560
You mount the Elastifile via
NFS and you're good to go.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:03.070
You put your data in and
you can start rendering.

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:05.007
It gives you a global namespace.

00:13:05.007 --> 00:13:07.090
You'll see at the end we're
going to launch around

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.550
3,000 nodes on the
cluster, so it's

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:11.050
going to shake it a little bit.

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:12.550
And all those
applications are going

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:14.183
to use the same namespace.

00:13:14.183 --> 00:13:15.850
So you won't have
having any limitations

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:18.760
into creating multiple
smaller volumes

00:13:18.760 --> 00:13:22.570
and having to manage all that,
that crazy number of volumes.

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:23.380
It scales.

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:25.630
So today if you
go on Marketplace,

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:28.000
and you'll see that we have
a managed service as well.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.030
You can start in just
a couple of terabytes

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:32.680
and scale it crazy.

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:36.130
You can go multiple petabytes
and as big as you want,

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:39.482
and shrink it back whenever
you don't need it anymore.

00:13:39.482 --> 00:13:41.440
And it has a whole bunch
of enterprise features

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:42.750
that are pretty cool.

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:45.583
Snapshots, we
dedupe, compression,

00:13:45.583 --> 00:13:47.500
we'll go into details
that a bit about tiering

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:51.190
to cloud storage so you don't
want to leave your data out

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.440
to the [INAUDIBLE] NFS
tier once you don't use it.

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:55.600
So we have a tiering
mechanism where

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:58.000
you're going to be able to
create policies to move them

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:01.150
onto cloud storage.

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:04.690
In a nutshell, really, a high
level of the architecture.

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:06.460
So the Elastifile
cloud file system

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:09.760
is going to be really the
cloud file system into GCP.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:11.200
It comes with some
of the modules,

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.870
as well, to be able
to move content

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.618
between your on prem
or other clouds to GCP

00:14:16.618 --> 00:14:17.410
and do the tiering.

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:20.540
So Cloud Connect is going to
be the module that you can use.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:23.410
It's a data mover, so it can
read pretty much anything

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:27.490
through NFS and move it to
cloud storage in Google.

00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:31.510
And then once you're ready to
work with your applications

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:33.730
in the cloud, you're going
to be able to move that,

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:37.570
to like hydrate the NFS
layer for your applications

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:41.050
to use the content on NFS.

00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:44.320
So Elastifile is going to
be installed on the Google

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:46.240
instances, and it's
going to take advantage

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:48.100
of the different types
of disks that you

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:53.620
have in instances, local SSDs,
PD-SSD, and standard PDs,

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:55.743
depending on what
kind of configuration

00:14:55.743 --> 00:14:57.160
you want, what
kind of performance

00:14:57.160 --> 00:15:01.030
you want out of this
storage as well.

00:15:01.030 --> 00:15:02.890
You can access it
through RESTful APIs

00:15:02.890 --> 00:15:05.810
as well to say, hey, I
want to create a volume.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:08.360
I want to be able to do a
sync between two clusters.

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:11.110
There's a very rich
API underneath that

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:13.360
allows you to script
it and write some code

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:14.620
around the applications.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:16.810
So we'll go a little
bit into more details

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:18.753
about different modules
and I'll give you

00:15:18.753 --> 00:15:20.170
some examples of
the architectures

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:24.787
that we support with those
types of modules [INAUDIBLE]..

00:15:24.787 --> 00:15:26.620
So really, when we
started to work with that

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:28.600
and defined what we were
going to do during the demo,

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:30.490
there's really two
types of architecture

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:34.840
when it comes to bursts and
moves the main media content

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:36.250
to the cloud and compute.

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:37.730
You have a hybrid architecture.

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:41.710
So some of your content
is-- well, it's on prem.

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:44.530
You have your NAS or you have
whatever [INAUDIBLE] on prem,

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:46.750
and you want to be able
to move it to the cloud,

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:50.120
launch a cluster, and be able
to compute and get it back.

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:51.850
So this is like a
typical architecture

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:53.620
you would see with
the Elastifile cloud

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:57.820
connect between your local NAS
and cloud storage in Google

00:15:57.820 --> 00:15:59.080
so you can transfer data.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:02.330
In the meantime, after,
before, it doesn't matter,

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:04.210
you can quickly launch
an Elastifile cluster.

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:05.960
And you'll see later
on that you can do it

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:08.530
from Marketplace as a
managed service in something

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:09.250
like Terraform.

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:11.170
We fully support
Terraform as well.

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:14.170
Whenever you're ready, you
hydrate on the NFS side,

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:17.260
you compute, and then you can
bring it back on prem whenever

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:18.260
you're finished with it.

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:22.120
So it's really a burst of
your computer to cloud.

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:24.070
You push it to the
cloud, you compute,

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:26.800
and you bring it back on prem.

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:29.152
Another pretty cool
architecture as well--

00:16:29.152 --> 00:16:30.610
and this is really
what we're going

00:16:30.610 --> 00:16:34.120
to demo at the end of the
session, is a virtual studio.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:36.220
So you're building a full
production environment

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:37.038
in the cloud.

00:16:37.038 --> 00:16:39.580
And what you have, really, on
your laptop and on your machine

00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:42.820
is just a super light
client like Teradici client,

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:44.560
for example, that
is super optimized

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:49.600
for graphical workflows,
and your Adobe stations,

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:52.888
your rendering stations, and
all of the compute for the media

00:16:52.888 --> 00:16:54.430
is going to be
installed in the cloud

00:16:54.430 --> 00:16:57.610
and taking advantage of that,
of Elastifile and the back end

00:16:57.610 --> 00:17:01.460
underneath to be able to
compute all that stuff.

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:04.452
So really the main difference
is that in that situation,

00:17:04.452 --> 00:17:06.619
you don't really transfer
much stuff into the cloud.

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:09.108
It's already there, or it's
been transferred before,

00:17:09.108 --> 00:17:11.650
and you're going to be able to
work pretty much from anywhere

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:14.990
on your content in the cloud.

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:17.400
All right, so again,
without going too crazy

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:19.550
into the details,
Elastifile is going

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:22.329
to be able to support multi-zone
and multi-region right away.

00:17:22.329 --> 00:17:26.250
So again, everywhere GCP is
available, we're available.

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:27.970
Multi-zone means
that at the top,

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.540
you have your applications,
all the different [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.123
nodes, they go through
a load balancer

00:17:32.123 --> 00:17:34.650
to write all the different
contents on to the storage

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:35.710
nodes at the bottom.

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:38.080
And those can be installed
in different regions.

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:40.920
So we do multiple copies of
the data into, in that example,

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:44.850
in West 1 with A, B, and C
so that if one of the regions

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:47.843
becomes available, your
application will still

00:17:47.843 --> 00:17:50.010
be able to access the data
through the load balancer

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:51.300
onto another region.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:54.180
And it's going to be
transparent for the application.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:57.480
Similar to that is
the multi-regions.

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:01.920
So what I forgot to mention
is that in the multi-zone

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:03.750
you do a sync copy of the data.

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:07.350
So when one of the
render node writes data,

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:09.150
it's going to be
acknowledged when

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:10.290
all the copies are
going to be written

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:11.332
into the different zones.

00:18:11.332 --> 00:18:14.820
With multi-region this is going
to be an async replication

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:18.510
into a different
zone for a DR mirror.

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:21.150
You have the production, you
have a mirror somewhere just

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:27.430
in case, that example,
West 1 because unavailable,

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:29.638
you can quickly
switch to Central 1

00:18:29.638 --> 00:18:32.180
and continue to compute in your
work onto a different region.

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:40.800
And that's done in Elastifile
through the management console.

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:43.050
ClearTier is really
that module that

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:46.380
allows you to do tiering
between your Elastifile

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:49.740
[INAUDIBLE] NFS layer and
cloud storage in Google.

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:52.193
And it can be manual or it
can be done through policies

00:18:52.193 --> 00:18:52.860
that you define.

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:56.580
Typically, all the files
then on the NFS layer that

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:59.500
haven't been touched in the
last three hours, for example,

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:01.643
move it to cloud storage.

00:19:01.643 --> 00:19:03.060
And a very cool
thing is that it's

00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:04.980
going to be transparent
to the application.

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:07.410
It's going to be present
in the namespace.

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:10.558
If the files is on the NFS
player or the object layer,

00:19:10.558 --> 00:19:11.850
same thing for the application.

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:15.060
Whenever it reads it, it's going
to read it from either cloud

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:17.400
storage or NFS,
depending on where

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:19.290
the file has been positioned.

00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:21.750
So it's pretty cool
to kind of reduce

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:23.430
the cost of the
infrastructure and be

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:25.950
able to move some of that
content to cloud storage

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:30.090
and even for use cases such as,
I don't know, OTT, streaming

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:32.387
content distribution,
using applications,

00:19:32.387 --> 00:19:34.470
then I'm going to use
object storage, for example,

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:37.037
and object protocols.

00:19:37.037 --> 00:19:39.120
CloudConnect, again, I've
talked about it already.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:41.370
It's really a data
mover that allows

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:43.910
you to move content
in GCP from your

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:46.260
on prem or from another cloud.

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.610
And it's going to
do a diff sync.

00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:50.610
So you can do the first
sync of your files.

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:52.620
It's going to move
everything in cloud storage,

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:56.797
and when you have files changing
on prem and you do a new sync,

00:19:56.797 --> 00:19:58.380
it's going to move
only the files that

00:19:58.380 --> 00:20:01.838
have been changed or added or
modified, things like that.

00:20:01.838 --> 00:20:03.630
And it's going to do
compression and dedupe

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:10.500
as well whenever transferring
the files to cloud storage.

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:12.570
I think the last one
on the features is--

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:14.778
we talked about-- you
mentioned that, the snapshot is

00:20:14.778 --> 00:20:18.060
really important whenever you
want to protect your data.

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:19.380
So we do support snapshots.

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:23.070
We have our snapshot
mechanism to disk and to GCS.

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:27.390
And what's pretty cool is that
in many enterprise storage

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:30.600
that you would see, not going to
name any one, but usually what

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:33.420
they do is that they reserve a
space on your tier one storage

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:35.790
to put the snapshots,
and that's what it is.

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:38.730
Like 10%, 30% of the
storage, the tier one storage

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:40.500
is going to be reserved
for the snapshot.

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:42.875
With Elastifile, it's going
to be a little bit different.

00:20:42.875 --> 00:20:46.170
You can take a snapshot, leave
it to disk for, I don't know,

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:47.760
a period of time
that you define,

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:51.630
and then it moves to GCS so that
you don't consume your tier one

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:54.210
storage with the snapshots.

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:57.330
Just taking a look
at the time here.

00:20:57.330 --> 00:21:01.950
We just announced last
week a managed service

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:04.320
in Google, which
is a service that

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:07.310
have been in beta since
the end of last year.

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:09.120
Managed service
means that it's going

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:11.880
to be fully embedded in Google,
in the UI, in the billing.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:13.380
And you're going
to launch it as you

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:16.710
would launch a Cloud SQL or a
BigQuery or things like that.

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:19.860
So in a matter of just a
few steps and a few clicks

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:23.340
you launch an Elastifile cluster
and you get an NFS [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:26.930
at the end, an IP,
and a shared volume,

00:21:26.930 --> 00:21:29.512
a volume that you can mount
and use on your application.

00:21:29.512 --> 00:21:31.470
And that's pretty cool,
because underneath it's

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:33.750
all going to be managed
by Google and Elastifile,

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:37.500
so you don't even see the
instances in the console,

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:39.480
in the Google console.

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:42.090
Just to give you an idea
of the pricing of that,

00:21:42.090 --> 00:21:45.780
it will go from $0.10 a gig
per month up to $0.30 a gig per

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:46.600
month.

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:48.150
So we have three
different models.

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:50.067
Really the difference
between the three models

00:21:50.067 --> 00:21:53.460
are the performance that you
require for your applications.

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:55.620
And it comes with different
types of clusters.

00:21:55.620 --> 00:21:57.520
So the minimum size of the
cluster that you can launch

00:21:57.520 --> 00:21:59.103
is going to be a
little bit different.

00:21:59.103 --> 00:22:02.010
So if you need super
high-speed performance,

00:22:02.010 --> 00:22:03.863
you would go to the
performance optimized.

00:22:03.863 --> 00:22:05.280
And if you need a
little bit less,

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:08.430
you would have a tendency to
go to the capacity optimized

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:11.250
for your apps.

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:14.010
Three ways to deploy
Elastifile today, really.

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:15.100
From the marketplace.

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:17.670
So you go there and you
do a search on Elastifile,

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:20.150
and then you launch it
as an unmanaged service,

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:21.900
meaning you're going
to see the instances.

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:24.750
It's up to you to add
instances, add capacity.

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:26.520
You can do it through
a managed service

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:28.770
that we just launched
GA this week,

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:31.453
but it's beta for
quite a while, where

00:22:31.453 --> 00:22:33.870
it's going to be super, super
easy for you in a few clicks

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:34.900
to launch it.

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:37.150
And we've been doing some
work with Terraform as well.

00:22:37.150 --> 00:22:39.358
I think there's going to be
a link at the end on that

00:22:39.358 --> 00:22:40.380
as well, like a QR code.

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:43.920
We did a tutorial to
launching a cluster,

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:46.920
adding capacity, shrinking
the cluster, and delete

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:49.290
the cluster, simply
with a Terraform script.

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:55.480
So we're going to take a
look at a little bit of that

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:58.722
in the demo as well, but we
have many different ways--

00:22:58.722 --> 00:22:59.680
well, many [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:02.410
We have a native GUI
in Elastifile for you

00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:07.000
to take a look at CPU
utilization, throughput, IOPS,

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:09.368
et cetera, et cetera,
because you want to track

00:23:09.368 --> 00:23:10.660
what's going on in the cluster.

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:11.290
If you have a--

00:23:11.290 --> 00:23:13.750
I don't know, a client that
is using all the bandwidth

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:16.580
or if you have a
node that went down.

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:20.950
So all that stuff is going to
be monitored into Elastifile

00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:23.590
for logging, for
updates, for being

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:27.640
able to monitor the resources,
reporting any specific issue

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:32.013
onto an agent, detect
anomalies, like a disk went down

00:23:32.013 --> 00:23:34.180
it's going to give you
alerts and all the good stuff

00:23:34.180 --> 00:23:38.440
that you would see
in a UI, all that

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:39.910
is going to be in Elastifile.

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:41.740
But then on top of that--

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:44.620
and we'll take a look at that in
the demo a little bit as well--

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:48.370
we have Grafana templates and
we set up Stackdriver as well so

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:51.910
that you can have a little bit
of a different and deeper view

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:54.520
of the stats, like how
many clients what's

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:56.800
the client that is using
the most bandwidth how

00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:59.570
many connections you have at
the same time in a cluster.

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:02.470
So it's enriching the
native Elastifile GUI

00:24:02.470 --> 00:24:06.840
with more monitoring
elements into GCP.

00:24:09.380 --> 00:24:11.960
Last but not least,
before we go to the demo,

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:15.050
just to give you an idea of the
performance of such a cluster,

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:18.780
because we talk about rendering
and editing and all that.

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:20.420
So to be able to
do that, you need

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:26.280
to be able to deliver throughput
and IOPS onto the storage.

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:29.780
So we worked a lot with
Google on doing some test

00:24:29.780 --> 00:24:31.800
on different clusters here.

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:34.030
So one is based on
PD-SSD, another one

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:36.650
is based on local
SSD and standard PD.

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:38.150
You will see that
the second one has

00:24:38.150 --> 00:24:41.090
got a dual application, yes,
because it's using local SSD.

00:24:41.090 --> 00:24:43.640
And the local SSD, whenever
you lose the instance

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:44.490
you lose the data.

00:24:44.490 --> 00:24:47.450
So we do multiple copies
on different instances

00:24:47.450 --> 00:24:51.170
so that if it happens
you don't lose the data.

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:54.468
So we're not going to go too
crazy into the details of which

00:24:54.468 --> 00:24:56.010
one is the best and
things like that,

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:58.760
but really to give
you a high level

00:24:58.760 --> 00:25:01.770
view of what it can deliver,
take a look, for example,

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:07.070
at the throughput with a read
on the four node configuration.

00:25:07.070 --> 00:25:08.960
You will get around
three gigabytes

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:10.770
per second of throughput.

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:13.790
And if you move
that to 32 nodes,

00:25:13.790 --> 00:25:17.120
it's going to reach nearly
17 gigabytes per second.

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:17.820
Meaning what?

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:19.195
Meaning that the
system is really

00:25:19.195 --> 00:25:21.710
going to scale in
capacity when you add

00:25:21.710 --> 00:25:24.210
nodes, but it's going to
scanning performance as well.

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:26.450
And in a near linear fashion.

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:27.320
Same for the IOPS.

00:25:27.320 --> 00:25:31.040
You can see the four nodes
is going to deliver 96k IOPS

00:25:31.040 --> 00:25:38.390
and up to 760k IOPS onto a 32
node configuration with PD-SSD.

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:40.940
Now if you move to local,
this is going to be faster.

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:47.000
The local SSDs are going to be
much faster than the PD-SSDs,

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:49.393
but again, it comes
with having to do

00:25:49.393 --> 00:25:50.810
multiple copies
on different nodes

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:54.260
to be able to sustain losing
an instance, for example.

00:25:54.260 --> 00:25:58.275
But look at the throughput here
onto a 32 node for the read

00:25:58.275 --> 00:25:58.820
and write.

00:25:58.820 --> 00:26:02.270
The read, typically, you're
reaching nearly 29 gigabytes

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:04.620
per second on the cluster.

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:06.350
So you can do a lot
of stuff with that,

00:26:06.350 --> 00:26:09.080
rendering and editing
and all the good stuff.

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:11.382
All right.

00:26:11.382 --> 00:26:12.090
Show, don't tell.

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:12.350
ADRIAN GRAHAM: All right.

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:13.130
YANNICK GUILLERM:
Let's take a look at--

00:26:13.130 --> 00:26:14.550
ADRIAN GRAHAM: After all
that telling, let's--

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:15.530
YANNICK GUILLERM: Yeah, exactly.

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:16.060
ADRIAN GRAHAM:
--do some showing.

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:18.310
YANNICK GUILLERM: Let's take
a look at the real stuff.

00:26:18.310 --> 00:26:21.900
ADRIAN GRAHAM: All
right, so first I'm

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:25.330
going to show you a
short video that we did.

00:26:25.330 --> 00:26:27.920
Last Next, if
anybody was here, you

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:30.745
might remember what we
call the robot dance party.

00:26:30.745 --> 00:26:31.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:35.750
So this is a
one-minute animation

00:26:35.750 --> 00:26:37.940
that we did as part of
what's called the Virtual

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:39.140
Studio or the cloud studio.

00:26:39.140 --> 00:26:40.730
And we did this at Next.

00:26:40.730 --> 00:26:42.980
Over the three days of
the show we actually

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:44.810
had artists working on
virtual workstations

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:47.240
at the show in the
booth doing things

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:50.270
like animating these robots
and lighting them and texturing

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:52.910
them and rendering them,
all totally on the cloud.

00:26:52.910 --> 00:26:55.880
Everybody had what's called
a zero client on their desk

00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:58.540
and a couple monitors,
keyboard, mouse, tablet,

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:01.050
and they were all working out
of the different remote data

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:05.152
center, some in
LA, some in Oregon,

00:27:05.152 --> 00:27:06.860
and there was even
one artist working out

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:09.080
of LA who was doing
like a video chat.

00:27:09.080 --> 00:27:11.150
You could-- there was
a blog post about this.

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:12.750
It was a lot of fun.

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:17.870
So we're going to use
these assets to just show

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:20.030
some of the capabilities
of Elastifile.

00:27:20.030 --> 00:27:22.160
and I'm going to connect
to a virtual workstation.

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:26.138
Just on my laptop here,
I've got a PC-over-IP client

00:27:26.138 --> 00:27:27.680
that I'm going to--
a software client

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:31.050
that I'm going to log into
my virtual workstation here.

00:27:31.050 --> 00:27:34.160
And this is located in our
LA data center, which is--

00:27:34.160 --> 00:27:35.330
I didn't test the ping.

00:27:35.330 --> 00:27:38.100
I think it's probably--

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:39.870
from the convention
center, from here

00:27:39.870 --> 00:27:42.900
it's probably like 25
milliseconds or something.

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:45.870
But I'm running a standard
Linux workstation that's

00:27:45.870 --> 00:27:49.110
running [INAUDIBLE] 7, and I've
got these scene files here.

00:27:49.110 --> 00:27:51.000
And I'm running an
application called Maya,

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:52.375
if anybody is
familiar with that.

00:27:52.375 --> 00:27:57.420
It's one of the main 3D
application packages out there.

00:27:57.420 --> 00:27:59.495
It's very heavily used
in VFX and animation.

00:27:59.495 --> 00:28:01.620
And here we've got all the
animation that's already

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:02.430
been baked and everything.

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:03.972
There's tons of
assets in this scene.

00:28:03.972 --> 00:28:07.020
Like I was mentioning
earlier, each of these robots

00:28:07.020 --> 00:28:10.380
is a separate asset that has
separate animation as an asset,

00:28:10.380 --> 00:28:12.330
and then all the
pieces of the set

00:28:12.330 --> 00:28:13.830
and the environment
and the lighting

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:15.372
and all the texturing
and everything.

00:28:15.372 --> 00:28:18.960
It's all separate, but it's
all combined into one scene

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:21.610
and rendered through
in a single frame.

00:28:21.610 --> 00:28:23.958
So let's look through
the main render camera,

00:28:23.958 --> 00:28:24.750
and we'll choose a.

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:28.547
Frame I think this guy's name
is-- these two robots that

00:28:28.547 --> 00:28:29.630
are named Fred and Ginger.

00:28:29.630 --> 00:28:31.020
I think this is Fred.

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:34.710
And so we're going to
click the Render button,

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:38.710
and I'll show you how elaborate
and complicated the process is.

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:40.837
So this is going to
render a single frame.

00:28:40.837 --> 00:28:42.670
And you'll see that it
blipped a little bit.

00:28:42.670 --> 00:28:44.780
That's because it's
calculating motion blur.

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:46.800
It's calculating where
the shutter opens

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:48.300
and where the shutter
closes, to use

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:53.160
sort of a metaphor of
an actual film camera.

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:55.320
So it's doing a whole lot
of computing of the scene

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:58.830
right now, and it's
going to build--

00:28:58.830 --> 00:29:00.350
we're rendering
in an application,

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:02.102
a renderer called V-Ray.

00:29:02.102 --> 00:29:03.810
V-Ray is by a company
called Chaos Group,

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:06.120
and we're pretty closely
partnered with them.

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:08.730
Actually, they have something
called Chaos Cloud, the V-Ray

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:10.407
Cloud, which runs on GCP.

00:29:10.407 --> 00:29:11.740
It's a render farm as a service.

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:13.198
There's a lot of
this-- by the way,

00:29:13.198 --> 00:29:17.750
you can see there's a demo
pod at the show, in, I think--

00:29:17.750 --> 00:29:19.453
there's like a
small media section.

00:29:19.453 --> 00:29:21.870
I actually haven't been there
because I got in last night.

00:29:21.870 --> 00:29:22.740
YANNICK GUILLERM: They
have the same setup.

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:23.700
ADRIAN GRAHAM: They
have the same setup,

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:26.370
so if you want to dive
deeper into this you can.

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:28.450
I want to see where
this render's at.

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:31.290
So I'm going to go and open my--

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:34.890
so it's actually rendering--
oh, I've got the window behind.

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:36.832
There we are.

00:29:36.832 --> 00:29:38.790
So anyways, it's performing
a render right now,

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:40.920
and you can see it's
working in the background.

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:43.920
I actually didn't pop
open the render viewer,

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:45.510
but there we are.

00:29:45.510 --> 00:29:47.220
So it's running this render.

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:48.850
And it's prepared all that data.

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:51.260
But it's only rendering
it inside this one square.

00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:53.670
So just give you an idea
of how complicated and

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:55.730
compute-intensive rendering
a single frame is,

00:29:55.730 --> 00:29:56.730
it's still not finished.

00:29:56.730 --> 00:29:58.855
And you can see it sort of
iterating over and over.

00:29:58.855 --> 00:30:01.050
It's called a
progressive renderer.

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:03.307
So these frames, by
the way, they take--

00:30:03.307 --> 00:30:04.890
I think the final
frames, some of them

00:30:04.890 --> 00:30:07.920
took from between
20 and 60 minutes

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.840
to render, depending
on what the content is.

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:12.360
Some frames are faster because
you have less in the frame.

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:14.700
Some take longer because maybe
there's more motion blur.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:17.280
Motion blur typically
requires more compute power

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:20.070
because it's got to
interpolate between all

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:22.070
the motion of the animation.

00:30:22.070 --> 00:30:25.203
Anyway, so that's one frame,
and not even a whole frame,

00:30:25.203 --> 00:30:26.370
and that's how long it took.

00:30:26.370 --> 00:30:28.163
And it was actually
pretty low resolution.

00:30:28.163 --> 00:30:30.330
So when it comes time to
render the whole sequence--

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:31.913
remember this is a
sequence of frames.

00:30:31.913 --> 00:30:36.360
I can grab the time slider
and slide through the duration

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:37.260
of the shot--

00:30:37.260 --> 00:30:39.177
if I want to send
that to a render farm,

00:30:39.177 --> 00:30:41.010
we're going to use
something called OpenCue,

00:30:41.010 --> 00:30:43.030
like I was mentioning earlier.

00:30:43.030 --> 00:30:47.700
So I'm just going to just
quickly set a couple of names

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:52.710
here, but I'm going to
define a few variables here.

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:54.360
I'm going to render
the default layer,

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:56.280
choose a camera to render.

00:30:56.280 --> 00:30:58.140
Usually you have
these pre-populated,

00:30:58.140 --> 00:30:59.790
but of course I don't.

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:02.610
And I'm going to select
my start and end frames.

00:31:02.610 --> 00:31:08.870
We'll do like frames
520 to 740, for example,

00:31:08.870 --> 00:31:11.370
and I can leave pretty
much everything else there.

00:31:11.370 --> 00:31:13.650
One important thing is what's
called this chunk size.

00:31:13.650 --> 00:31:17.250
That's how many frames to render
per job that gets deployed.

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:19.010
So when I'm done,
I'm just going to--

00:31:19.010 --> 00:31:21.940
my screen resolution has changed
since the last time I ran this.

00:31:21.940 --> 00:31:24.630
I'm going to click Submit and
it's saying Job Submitted.

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:27.810
So now I can go to what's
called a cue commander

00:31:27.810 --> 00:31:32.850
and look at all the jobs under
the cue commander, and open--

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:34.740
sorry, now I just quit.

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:36.060
I didn't mean to do that.

00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:38.520
I'll just relaunch it.

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:41.810
Cue commander, I'll
launch the cue GUI.

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:44.257
And this is where I could
start to see all of my-- here,

00:31:44.257 --> 00:31:45.840
there's the job that
I just submitted.

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:47.810
If I double click that,
these are all my frames

00:31:47.810 --> 00:31:49.070
that have been cued up.

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:53.480
All right, so pretty simply, we
just cued up hundreds of frames

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:55.430
and they're all
organized and managed

00:31:55.430 --> 00:31:57.810
and they're all reading
off the Elastifile.

00:31:57.810 --> 00:32:00.290
So all that data-- by the
way, this virtual workstation

00:32:00.290 --> 00:32:03.590
has Elastifile mounted under
the slash projects namespace.

00:32:03.590 --> 00:32:06.527
So everything is hitting
that slash projects mount

00:32:06.527 --> 00:32:08.360
point, that volume,
including all the render

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:10.170
workers and everything else.

00:32:10.170 --> 00:32:11.900
So this is one job.

00:32:11.900 --> 00:32:13.782
And that's great, but
I'm just one person.

00:32:13.782 --> 00:32:14.990
What if I was a whole studio?

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:17.900
What if I was a whole--

00:32:17.900 --> 00:32:20.450
hundreds of artists
working in a studio?

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:24.470
Well, I can deploy
many thousands of jobs

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:30.240
to sort of artificially fake the
workload of a studio out there.

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:33.480
And you can see, I've got
three managed instance groups,

00:32:33.480 --> 00:32:35.510
each with 1,000 render
workers in them.

00:32:35.510 --> 00:32:36.620
And I'm going to deploy--

00:32:36.620 --> 00:32:39.530
I've got a script
that will deploy

00:32:39.530 --> 00:32:41.960
hundreds and hundreds
of jobs and just fill up

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:43.310
the farm instantly.

00:32:43.310 --> 00:32:48.860
So it's-- I actually
forget, it's cue renders--

00:32:48.860 --> 00:32:50.540
no, I'm forgetting the code.

00:32:50.540 --> 00:32:55.700
Dash project all--
no, it's dash--

00:32:55.700 --> 00:32:59.360
dash project, sorry, equals all.

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:01.242
So watch this.

00:33:01.242 --> 00:33:03.700
You're going to see a whole
bunch of jobs getting launched.

00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:05.740
You might see a couple
alembic errors or something

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:07.698
because it's just
substituting-- there he goes.

00:33:07.698 --> 00:33:08.890
You could ignore those.

00:33:08.890 --> 00:33:12.320
But this is all just command
line submitting these jobs.

00:33:12.320 --> 00:33:15.138
And while these are being
submitted, now we can watch--

00:33:15.138 --> 00:33:15.680
look at this.

00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:17.800
We've got all these
jobs just being cued up

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:22.190
and the farm is being stuffed
full of all these tasks.

00:33:22.190 --> 00:33:25.520
So each job, you could see,
consists of hundreds of frames.

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:28.900
For example, you can
just reload and you

00:33:28.900 --> 00:33:30.800
can see there's all
these different jobs.

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:33.675
And this is a wide
variety of jobs.

00:33:33.675 --> 00:33:35.050
So right now is
actually probably

00:33:35.050 --> 00:33:36.613
a good time to go and look at--

00:33:36.613 --> 00:33:38.780
YANNICK GUILLERM: Take a
look at the cluster itself.

00:33:38.780 --> 00:33:39.910
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Look
at the cluster itself.

00:33:39.910 --> 00:33:42.535
So I'll let you take over and go
through some of the monitoring

00:33:42.535 --> 00:33:43.100
tools.

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:44.017
YANNICK GUILLERM: So--

00:33:44.017 --> 00:33:45.683
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Just
click in the middle.

00:33:45.683 --> 00:33:46.355
I'm sorry.

00:33:46.355 --> 00:33:47.513
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:33:47.513 --> 00:33:48.680
YANNICK GUILLERM: All right.

00:33:48.680 --> 00:33:49.360
No, no.

00:33:49.360 --> 00:33:50.380
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Just
click there and click

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:51.590
in the middle of the window.

00:33:51.590 --> 00:33:52.090
Great.

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:53.340
Right there, just right there.

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:54.695
OK, there you go.

00:33:54.695 --> 00:33:55.570
YANNICK GUILLERM: OK.

00:33:55.570 --> 00:33:57.070
So we're going to
be connect-- we're

00:33:57.070 --> 00:33:59.750
connected to the clusters,
to the Elastifile cluster

00:33:59.750 --> 00:34:00.250
right now.

00:34:00.250 --> 00:34:02.822
So that's what the
GUI looks like.

00:34:02.822 --> 00:34:05.030
There's going to be lots of
different things in there

00:34:05.030 --> 00:34:07.322
and we're not going to go
into details of all of those.

00:34:07.322 --> 00:34:09.370
But we have a bunch
of data containers

00:34:09.370 --> 00:34:11.239
that have been created.

00:34:11.239 --> 00:34:13.120
This is for some of
the tools that we

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:15.230
use for those render nodes.

00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:18.100
This is the main
one, the ECFS render.

00:34:18.100 --> 00:34:23.360
So there's not crazy amount
of data on the volume right

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:24.190
outlet.

00:34:24.190 --> 00:34:25.780
For each of those
containers you can

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:30.790
set soft quotas and hard
quotas as well for NFS quotas.

00:34:30.790 --> 00:34:32.969
On those shares,
you're also going

00:34:32.969 --> 00:34:35.739
to be able to define if
you want to do snapshots,

00:34:35.739 --> 00:34:39.199
if you want to be able
to do tiering to objects.

00:34:39.199 --> 00:34:43.150
We haven't set it up here to be
able to move data to the cloud

00:34:43.150 --> 00:34:46.153
storage, but this is something
that you would do in here.

00:34:46.153 --> 00:34:48.070
You want to do dedupe,
compressions, you know,

00:34:48.070 --> 00:34:49.810
things like that.

00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:53.199
On the main window here, we
will probably [INAUDIBLE] you

00:34:53.199 --> 00:34:56.090
will see a peak of
content coming in.

00:34:56.090 --> 00:34:57.470
So it means that
we're seeing now

00:34:57.470 --> 00:34:59.720
all the jobs that have been
launched at the same time.

00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:00.750
I don't know how many
jobs you launched.

00:35:00.750 --> 00:35:02.050
It's a total off--

00:35:02.050 --> 00:35:04.540
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Well, there's
like 120-something jobs,

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:07.360
but each job has
hundreds of frames.

00:35:07.360 --> 00:35:07.930
Sub jobs.

00:35:07.930 --> 00:35:09.010
YANNICK GUILLERM: See
this, for example?

00:35:09.010 --> 00:35:10.570
This is going to track
the number of connections

00:35:10.570 --> 00:35:12.530
that you have on the
cluster at the same time.

00:35:12.530 --> 00:35:15.130
So we have a lot
of clients, each

00:35:15.130 --> 00:35:19.060
of them mounting the Elastifile
NFS volume at the same time,

00:35:19.060 --> 00:35:20.650
so they're all
reading and writing

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:22.590
that content on the
cluster at the same time.

00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:23.590
ADRIAN GRAHAM: See,
it's climbing as we're--

00:35:23.590 --> 00:35:26.048
YANNICK GUILLERM: Yeah, it's
climbing because the processes

00:35:26.048 --> 00:35:27.880
are running and getting kicked.

00:35:27.880 --> 00:35:30.760
So you can see here
that the cluster

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:34.480
is getting hammered a little
bit with throughput and IOPS.

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:39.460
We're around 1.2 gigabytes
per second during the work

00:35:39.460 --> 00:35:40.975
that we didn't do
to do that demo,

00:35:40.975 --> 00:35:42.850
on that three-node
cluster, because it's only

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:47.920
a three-node cluster, we went
to about 2.3 or 2.2 gigabytes

00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:50.680
per second read for
thousands and thousands

00:35:50.680 --> 00:35:53.560
of processes running at
the same time on a cluster.

00:35:53.560 --> 00:35:56.883
So this is going to give you
a nice view of the cluster.

00:35:56.883 --> 00:35:58.300
But if you want a
little bit more,

00:35:58.300 --> 00:36:02.080
deeper details on what's going
on, Grafana is a good example.

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:04.930
Stackdriver is also
a good way to take

00:36:04.930 --> 00:36:08.800
a look at what's going on and
statistics on the cluster.

00:36:08.800 --> 00:36:12.220
Grafana, typically, will give
you information on latency,

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:13.453
number of IOPS.

00:36:13.453 --> 00:36:14.870
This is something
that you set up.

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:16.270
We're going to give
you some templates

00:36:16.270 --> 00:36:17.260
to be able to see that.

00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:19.270
But see, for example,
yesterday night

00:36:19.270 --> 00:36:22.185
you were not sleeping and
playing with that stuff,

00:36:22.185 --> 00:36:24.440
you launched massive
amounts of nodes.

00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:26.950
And at a point we
had 3,000 clients

00:36:26.950 --> 00:36:28.810
running on the same
time in the cluster.

00:36:28.810 --> 00:36:34.060
And we actually reached
around 1.6 or 1.78 gigabytes

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:37.120
per second read
throughput on the cluster.

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:39.000
So this is going
to be very useful

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:40.750
for you to take a look
at what's going on,

00:36:40.750 --> 00:36:45.028
if you want a little bit of
a deeper look on the clients

00:36:45.028 --> 00:36:47.320
that are connected onto the
cluster, which one is using

00:36:47.320 --> 00:36:49.660
what, which one is using
throughput or eating

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:52.210
all your bandwidth
and things like that.

00:36:52.210 --> 00:36:54.460
You're going to be able to
do that through Stackdriver

00:36:54.460 --> 00:36:56.320
and be able to track
the activity on all

00:36:56.320 --> 00:36:59.230
the different clients that are
running onto the cluster, which

00:36:59.230 --> 00:37:01.840
are, right now, I don't know
how many we have right now,

00:37:01.840 --> 00:37:04.390
many connections, but
just yeah, we're climbing.

00:37:04.390 --> 00:37:07.623
So it's launching a lot of
stuff on the cluster right now.

00:37:07.623 --> 00:37:10.040
And again, we don't have time
to go into the crazy detail,

00:37:10.040 --> 00:37:13.917
that this is going to give
you as well here the nodes,

00:37:13.917 --> 00:37:16.000
actually, the [INAUDIBLE],,
the storage nodes that

00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:18.440
are used for the rendering.

00:37:18.440 --> 00:37:21.760
So the load balancer
on top of Elastifile

00:37:21.760 --> 00:37:24.640
is going to fully
manage, fully balance

00:37:24.640 --> 00:37:28.090
the load of all the
different compute process

00:37:28.090 --> 00:37:29.030
on the three nodes.

00:37:29.030 --> 00:37:31.060
So it's going to distribute
that on to reading

00:37:31.060 --> 00:37:35.030
and writing onto multiple
disks into the cluster.

00:37:35.030 --> 00:37:37.060
And you see here some
of the information

00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:40.270
of how much a node is
going to give you in terms

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:42.700
of performance on the cluster.

00:37:42.700 --> 00:37:44.100
ADRIAN GRAHAM: I'm just going to
show a couple of other things.

00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:46.517
There's a couple of other
things that are already running,

00:37:46.517 --> 00:37:49.310
or that are running right now.

00:37:49.310 --> 00:37:51.190
So remember I mentioned
fluid simulations?

00:37:51.190 --> 00:37:54.680
Well, I've got a
massive ultramem machine

00:37:54.680 --> 00:37:59.570
with 160 vCPUs running
five concurrent fluid sims

00:37:59.570 --> 00:38:01.250
that I'll show you in a second.

00:38:01.250 --> 00:38:03.160
But this is all just
writing the data, again,

00:38:03.160 --> 00:38:05.740
to the Elastifile cluster,
right, to the same namespace

00:38:05.740 --> 00:38:06.890
as everything else.

00:38:06.890 --> 00:38:09.640
And I'm able to, on my virtual
workstation, directly read

00:38:09.640 --> 00:38:10.187
that data.

00:38:10.187 --> 00:38:12.520
This is the results of that
fluid simulation and reading

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:14.250
that data live off that ECFS.

00:38:14.250 --> 00:38:17.830
And these are about
24, 25 million points.

00:38:17.830 --> 00:38:21.220
So obviously you can't
scrub in real time,

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:25.390
just because it's got to load
all the data frame by frame,

00:38:25.390 --> 00:38:29.500
but it's hitting it pretty
hard to be able to visualize

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:31.180
all this information.

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:36.050
And then in turn, I can use a
different application like Maya

00:38:36.050 --> 00:38:38.540
to render all that data.

00:38:38.540 --> 00:38:42.710
So this virtual workstation
has, obviously GPU attached.

00:38:42.710 --> 00:38:47.240
It's got one of our new Nvidia,
the touring class GPUs, the T4,

00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:51.920
and that enables me to render
in certain renders which

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:53.750
are GPU-capable.

00:38:53.750 --> 00:38:59.540
So I can render this scene by
going into this V-Ray IPR here.

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:02.480
And it's going to render
locally on the GPU.

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:04.968
Again, all this data is
stored on the Elastifile, all

00:39:04.968 --> 00:39:07.010
that mesh data that you
saw there earlier and all

00:39:07.010 --> 00:39:08.190
the animation.

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:12.420
So this is rendering
on a single T4.

00:39:12.420 --> 00:39:14.240
So it's really
hammering that GPU.

00:39:14.240 --> 00:39:20.900
That's the local T4 GPU, but
I could have an entire cluster

00:39:20.900 --> 00:39:21.980
of GPUs as well.

00:39:21.980 --> 00:39:26.270
And V-Ray has the ability to
distribute its render workload

00:39:26.270 --> 00:39:29.430
to multiple machines that
all have GPUs attached.

00:39:29.430 --> 00:39:31.280
So if I turn on
distributed rendering,

00:39:31.280 --> 00:39:33.830
I can connect to
all these machines

00:39:33.830 --> 00:39:37.430
as part of my GCP project.

00:39:37.430 --> 00:39:41.000
And each of those machines has
four GPUs attached as well.

00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:45.020
So this is addressing
a total of 40 GPUs.

00:39:45.020 --> 00:39:47.090
These are all
reading and writing

00:39:47.090 --> 00:39:48.800
to the Elastifile
cluster, again,

00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:50.717
because they all just
have the same outpoints.

00:39:50.717 --> 00:39:52.120
It's just a Posix file system.

00:39:52.120 --> 00:39:53.890
YANNICK GUILLERM: A shared
cluster used for many things.

00:39:53.890 --> 00:39:55.432
ADRIAN GRAHAM: It's
a shared cluster,

00:39:55.432 --> 00:39:58.190
it's just a Posix file system
that they mount as NFS.

00:39:58.190 --> 00:40:01.830
And what this is doing is--

00:40:01.830 --> 00:40:03.050
where's my RDP?

00:40:03.050 --> 00:40:06.650
So you could see it's connecting
to all those remote machines

00:40:06.650 --> 00:40:08.760
and, give it a
second, there we are.

00:40:08.760 --> 00:40:10.740
So now we're
rendering on 40 GPUs,

00:40:10.740 --> 00:40:13.190
and you can see it's a lot
faster when I tumble around.

00:40:13.190 --> 00:40:14.565
And this is an
interactive scene.

00:40:14.565 --> 00:40:17.160
I could tweak the
shading, add lights,

00:40:17.160 --> 00:40:20.630
change the time, everything,
and it'll just resolve.

00:40:20.630 --> 00:40:22.580
So this is sending a
lot of data and it's

00:40:22.580 --> 00:40:26.570
hitting the Elastifile really,
really hard, trying to absorb

00:40:26.570 --> 00:40:28.490
and slurping all
this data into memory

00:40:28.490 --> 00:40:31.460
so that it can compute
it across all these GPUs

00:40:31.460 --> 00:40:33.810
and on the display here.

00:40:33.810 --> 00:40:38.090
So let's go back
to the presentation

00:40:38.090 --> 00:40:41.043
here, because I think we're
in the homestretch here.

00:40:41.043 --> 00:40:41.960
YANNICK GUILLERM: Yes.

00:40:41.960 --> 00:40:44.270
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Let's look
at what you just saw,

00:40:44.270 --> 00:40:47.720
the architecture of this
demo that you just saw.

00:40:47.720 --> 00:40:49.370
We have all these
render workers,

00:40:49.370 --> 00:40:51.287
we have all these sim
workers, and they're all

00:40:51.287 --> 00:40:53.150
hitting the Elastifile,
but we're literally

00:40:53.150 --> 00:40:56.900
writing gigabytes of
cache files to these--

00:40:56.900 --> 00:41:00.050
you saw that 160 vCPU
ultramem machine.

00:41:00.050 --> 00:41:04.340
Each frame is writing about,
I think, 20 or 30 gigs

00:41:04.340 --> 00:41:05.480
per simulation.

00:41:05.480 --> 00:41:06.620
That's times five.

00:41:06.620 --> 00:41:09.440
So that's being
written concurrently.

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:13.100
We've probably climbed to
3,000 connections now--

00:41:13.100 --> 00:41:16.640
actually, got more connections
because of all the GPU

00:41:16.640 --> 00:41:17.637
render workers.

00:41:17.637 --> 00:41:18.970
And then we're visualizing this.

00:41:18.970 --> 00:41:20.780
We've got a virtual
workstation, and you

00:41:20.780 --> 00:41:23.900
could have an entire
facility full of people,

00:41:23.900 --> 00:41:25.790
full of artists on
virtual workstations

00:41:25.790 --> 00:41:27.770
all hitting the same
storage appliance, which

00:41:27.770 --> 00:41:29.370
is pretty impressive.

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:30.612
And so let's talk about--

00:41:30.612 --> 00:41:31.820
YANNICK GUILLERM: Yeah, sure.

00:41:31.820 --> 00:41:34.460
So what's next,
because for you guys

00:41:34.460 --> 00:41:36.500
to be able to take a
look at that and test it

00:41:36.500 --> 00:41:41.360
and there's different ways
you could use to play with.

00:41:41.360 --> 00:41:44.280
We've built a solution Google to
be able to, like I said before,

00:41:44.280 --> 00:41:46.190
to announce our
cluster with Terraform.

00:41:46.190 --> 00:41:48.860
So in just changing few
variables into a Terraform

00:41:48.860 --> 00:41:51.770
script, you can launch your
cluster, a minimum cluster,

00:41:51.770 --> 00:41:54.320
add some nodes, so add
capacity and performance,

00:41:54.320 --> 00:41:55.880
and then shrink the
cluster whenever

00:41:55.880 --> 00:41:58.100
you don't need all those
nodes anymore because you

00:41:58.100 --> 00:41:59.840
had to peak into rendering.

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:02.540
You can shrink its back to
its minimum configuration

00:42:02.540 --> 00:42:05.290
and delete the cluster whenever
you don't need it anymore.

00:42:05.290 --> 00:42:07.790
You'll be able to launch it
from Marketplace as well, like I

00:42:07.790 --> 00:42:09.680
said before,
nonmanaged, unmanaged,

00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:11.180
so it's going to
be really up to you

00:42:11.180 --> 00:42:14.900
to add instances or shrink
the cluster yourself.

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:18.740
If you want a full managed
experience with the GCP

00:42:18.740 --> 00:42:21.200
console, we have that
as well, and something

00:42:21.200 --> 00:42:22.190
that went GA this week.

00:42:22.190 --> 00:42:24.590
So we're pretty happy about it.

00:42:24.590 --> 00:42:27.380
You can have, really, the
Elastifile in the console

00:42:27.380 --> 00:42:28.288
itself.

00:42:28.288 --> 00:42:30.830
No matter if you click, you're
going to be able to launch it.

00:42:30.830 --> 00:42:35.360
And I encourage you as well to
take a look at the Quicklab.

00:42:35.360 --> 00:42:38.960
Maybe you're familiar with
that company called Quicklab

00:42:38.960 --> 00:42:40.610
for building some labs.

00:42:40.610 --> 00:42:44.660
We built two labs, one on
launching a smaller Elastifile

00:42:44.660 --> 00:42:46.123
cluster, get familiar with it.

00:42:46.123 --> 00:42:47.540
This is really a
cool one as well.

00:42:47.540 --> 00:42:51.110
We built one with Blender to
do a rendering in Quicklab.

00:42:51.110 --> 00:42:52.790
So what's really
cool with Quicklab

00:42:52.790 --> 00:42:56.150
is that it's going to build
the entire Google console

00:42:56.150 --> 00:42:58.260
environment for
you, the project,

00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:00.770
it's going to give you the
user and all that good stuff.

00:43:00.770 --> 00:43:03.340
So play with it.

00:43:03.340 --> 00:43:04.790
And I think we're--

00:43:04.790 --> 00:43:05.623
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Yeah.

00:43:05.623 --> 00:43:08.510
So if you want to learn
more about OpenCue,

00:43:08.510 --> 00:43:11.270
you want to hightail it to the
Moscone Center, breakout room

00:43:11.270 --> 00:43:12.343
four.

00:43:12.343 --> 00:43:13.760
YANNICK GUILLERM:
Gotta run there.

00:43:13.760 --> 00:43:16.040
ADRIAN GRAHAM: My colleague
Brendan Doyle and Greg

00:43:16.040 --> 00:43:19.730
Denton will be doing a
deeper dive into OpenCue.

00:43:19.730 --> 00:43:22.210
And finally, there
is a new entry

00:43:22.210 --> 00:43:24.920
to our cloud
Marketplace by Nvidia.

00:43:24.920 --> 00:43:28.820
Nvidia now has a deployable
virtual workstation

00:43:28.820 --> 00:43:32.690
that's somewhat managed in that
it takes care of all the driver

00:43:32.690 --> 00:43:34.690
installation and updates
and everything for you.

00:43:34.690 --> 00:43:38.920
So that is leveraging the
T4, which is our latest GPU.

00:43:38.920 --> 00:43:40.930
We also offer P4s and P100s.

